<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed/notarized Monitra.app.

    These are only consulted when the bundle is actually code-signed with a
    Developer ID identity (see BUILD.md "Signing"). An unsigned local build
    ignores this file entirely.

    Each entitlement below is present because a PyInstaller-packaged Python
    application does not start under the hardened runtime without it, not
    because it sounded useful. Nothing here grants Monitra a *capability*:
    Screen Recording and Input Monitoring are TCC permissions the user grants
    at runtime, prompted by the NSUsageDescription strings in Info.plist, and
    cannot be claimed by entitlement.

    Deliberately absent:

      com.apple.security.app-sandbox
          The App Sandbox forbids the global input monitoring and window-list
          access this application exists to do. Sandboxed distribution would
          mean shipping a build that silently tracks nothing. Monitra is
          distributed directly (Developer ID + notarization), not through the
          Mac App Store, which is the supported path for this class of app.

      com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
          CPython here runs no JIT and allocates no executable memory. These
          are the two entitlements most often copy-pasted into Python bundles
          without need; both materially weaken the hardened runtime, so they
          stay out until something actually fails without them.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        PyInstaller collects Qt and CPython shared libraries into the bundle
        and re-signs them with the same Developer ID identity as the app. That
        is a different team/identity chain from Apple's platform libraries, so
        strict library validation refuses to load them and the app dies at
        launch with "code signature invalid". This is the one entitlement a
        PyInstaller bundle genuinely cannot start without.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        The bundled interpreter is started with PyInstaller's own
        DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH pointing inside Contents/Frameworks. The hardened
        runtime scrubs DYLD_* variables unless this is set, after which the
        app launches but cannot find its own Qt libraries.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        Outbound HTTPS to the configured backend, and nothing else. Monitra
        listens on no port and accepts no incoming connection, so the
        server-side network entitlement is not requested.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
